\title{[GCD2] GCD of consecutive Fibonacci numbers (2 pts)}
\date{2026-09-01T16:02:01Z}
\tag{algorithms}
\tag{problem}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{Recall the [Fibonacci numbers](00BL), defined by #{F_0 = 0}, #{F_1
= 1}, and #{F_{n+2} = F_{n+1} + F_n}. That is, the first two Fibonacci
numbers are #{0, 1}, and then each subsequent Fibonacci number is the
sum of the previous two, so the first few are #{0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8,
13, \dots}}

\ol{
  \li{Compute #{F_9} and #{F_{10}}, and trace the execution of the [Euclidean Algorithm](00F9) to compute #{\gcd(F_{10}, F_9)}. What happens?}
  \li{Prove by induction on #{n} that #{\gcd(F_{n+1}, F_n) = 1} for all #{n \geq 0}.}
  \li{Explain why your proof also shows that the Euclidean Algorithm requires #{n} recursive steps to compute #{\gcd(F_{n+1}, F_n) = 1}.}
}

